---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { DATA_LICENSE, REPO_URL, absUrl, dataUrl, site } from '../../lib/site';

const s = site();
const example = s.gamesById.get('Nintendo/NES/us/Mega_Man') ?? s.games[0];
const exampleJson = JSON.stringify(example, null, 2);
const gamesUrl = absUrl('data/v1/games.json');
const fetchExample = `const res = await fetch('${gamesUrl}');
const { games } = await res.json();

// Index by id for O(1) lookups from your own collection records.
const byId = new Map(games.map((g) => [g.id, g]));
byId.get('Nintendo/NES/us/Mega_Man').released.sortable; // "1987-12-01"`;
const collectionExample = `// Your app's table only needs to store the GameDB id.
{ "user": 42, "game": "Nintendo/NES/us/Mega_Man", "condition": "CIB" }`;

const files = [
  ['index.json', 'Manifest: version, build time, counts and links to every file below.'],
  ['games.json', `Every game (${s.games.length.toLocaleString('en-US')}) as one array, denormalized with publisher and developer names.`],
  ['games.csv', 'The same data as CSV. Multiple developers are separated by ";".'],
  ['platforms.json', 'Company → platform → region tree with game counts.'],
  ['publishers.json', 'Publishers with display names and the ids of their games.'],
  ['developers.json', 'Developers with display names and the ids of their games.'],
  ['aliases.json', 'Retired ids mapped to their replacements.'],
  ['schema/game.schema.json', 'JSON Schema for a game object.']
];
---
<Base title="Data & API" description="Download the GameDB catalog as JSON, CSV or XLSX and use it in your own app.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Data & API' }]} />
  <h1>Data for developers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The whole catalog is published as public-domain (CC0) static files that you can fetch from your app or download and bundle.
    No API key and no rate limits. The files are regenerated every time the catalog changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifiers</h2>
  <p>
    Every game has an <code>id</code> of the form <code>&lt;company&gt;/&lt;platform&gt;/&lt;region&gt;/&lt;guid&gt;</code>,
    for example <code>{example.id}</code>. It is unique across the catalog and stable, so use it as the foreign key in your own
    collection tables. A <code>guid</code> on its own is only unique within one region; the same title on two platforms has two ids.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{collectionExample}</code></pre>
  <p>
    If an id ever has to change (a typo, or two duplicate records merged), the old id is listed in
    <a href={dataUrl('aliases.json')}><code>aliases.json</code></a>, so you can migrate stored references.
  </p>

  <h2>Files</h2>
  <p>All paths are under <code>{absUrl('data/v1/')}</code>.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {files.map(([f, d]) => (
          <tr><td class="nowrap"><a href={dataUrl(f)}><code>{f}</code></a></td><td>{d}</td></tr>
        ))}
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><code>platforms/&lt;company&gt;/&lt;platform&gt;/&lt;region&gt;.json</code><br /><code>.csv</code> · <code>.xlsx</code></td>
          <td>One region's games. For example, <a href={dataUrl('platforms/Nintendo/NES/us.json')}><code>platforms/Nintendo/NES/us.json</code></a>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="nowrap"><code>games/&lt;id&gt;.json</code></td>
          <td>A single game. For example, <a href={dataUrl(`games/${example.id}.json`)}><code>games/{example.id}.json</code></a>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Game object</h2>
  <pre><code>{exampleJson}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>released</code> is <code>null</code> when unknown. <code>month</code> and <code>day</code> are omitted when unknown, and <code>sortable</code> fills them in with <code>01</code> so you can sort by it as a string.</li>
    <li><code>publisher</code> is <code>null</code> when unknown, and <code>developers</code> can be empty. Some records are still being filled in.</li>
    <li>Fields may be added within <code>v1</code>. Removing or renaming a field will only happen in a new version path.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <pre><code>{fetchExample}</code></pre>

  <h2>License</h2>
  <p>
    The data is dedicated to the public domain under <a href={DATA_LICENSE.url}>{DATA_LICENSE.name}</a>.
    You can copy it, change it, bundle it in commercial apps and redistribute it with no attribution required.
    Every JSON file carries a <code>license</code> field so this stays attached to downloaded copies.
    Credit and a link back are appreciated but not required.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">The site and tooling code is licensed separately under MIT.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    The source of truth is the JSON in the <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub repository</a>. Each game page links to its record, so you can suggest a fix
    with a pull request. By contributing data you agree to release it under CC0.
  </p>
</Base>
